title: Why only measured codecs warn as slow
tags: [[file panel]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
aliases: slow-decoder av1 dav1d
summary: A decoder can be present and unusable — AV1 through libaom — so the probe warns; but only for a codec whose fallback was measured too slow, since a warning on files that play fine is worse than none.

A ''present'' decoder is not a ''usable'' one. `gst-discoverer-1.0` reports nothing
missing, the element is created, the file plays — at a few frames per second. And the
same file plays perfectly in mpv, which decodes with ffmpeg where the GUI decodes with
whatever GStreamer element WebKit picks. Nothing on screen explained it.

So `GET /__media-probe` also answers `slow`: a message when the codec's ''fallback''
decoder is known to be slow, no fast one is installed, and the stream's pixel rate
(width × height × frames per second) is over a budget. The [[file panel]] shows it
above the video and plays the file anyway — the user may only want to look through
it.

The judgement is narrow on purpose. A codec is listed in `SLOW_FALLBACKS`
(`crates/gui/src/media_support.rs`) only once it has been measured. One so far:

|!Codec |!Fallback |!Fast decoders |!Package |
|AV1 |libaom `av1dec` |`dav1ddec`, `avdec_av1`, the VA-API and NVDEC ones |`gst-plugin-dav1d` |

`av1dec` is the only AV1 decoder of `gst-plugins-bad`, and it is single-threaded. On
a 4-core machine it decoded a 3840×2160 stream at 25 frames per second (about
207 Mpx/s) where dav1d reached 75 through ffmpeg — under the 29.97 the file needs,
before the web view converts each frame to RGBA and composites it. The budget is
150 Mpx/s: 1080p60 (124) stays silent, 1440p60 and 4K30 (221 and 248) warn. A codec
whose fallback is a threaded libav decoder does not belong in the table.

Two details follow from what a wrong answer costs:

* a decoder whose presence cannot be determined counts as ''present'' — no warning. For the output elements it is the reverse, since there a wrong ''present'' is a crash ([[Why a media file is not played on sight]]);
* `slow` is never set when a decoder is missing: there is no playback to be slow.
